1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,750
You push a little handle, the water swirls, and it's gone.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:05,978
Most of us never think about it again.

3
00:00:05,978 --> 00:00:07,582
But gone isn't a place.

4
00:00:08,032 --> 00:00:10,928
That water just dropped into a network of

5
00:00:10,928 --> 00:00:13,824
pipes running under almost every street in America.

6
00:00:13,824 --> 00:00:17,121
Within hours, most of it will be cleaned and sent

7
00:00:17,121 --> 00:00:20,088
back to a river, a lake, or the ocean.

8
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:23,704
When the local paper in Vancouver, Washington, followed

9
00:00:23,704 --> 00:00:26,857
the water through the city's plant, raw sewage

10
00:00:26,857 --> 00:00:29,617
to reasonably clean took about eight hours.

11
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:31,757
And here's the strange part.

12
00:00:31,757 --> 00:00:33,653
The hardest worker in that plant

13
00:00:33,653 --> 00:00:35,548
isn't a pump or a filter.

14
00:00:35,548 --> 00:00:38,886
It's a living crowd you can't even see, and the plant

15
00:00:38,886 --> 00:00:41,920
has to keep it fed and breathing around the clock.

16
00:00:42,394 --> 00:00:45,442
So let's follow one flush, start to finish.

17
00:00:45,442 --> 00:00:49,709
Five stops, a side business that turns the leftovers into fuel

18
00:00:49,709 --> 00:00:53,975
and fertilizer, and at the end, a place in California where

19
00:00:53,975 --> 00:00:57,466
this water heads back toward the drinking water supply.

20
00:00:57,466 --> 00:00:59,627
And I'll show you how they get it that clean.

21
00:01:00,546 --> 00:01:02,371
Stop one, the pipes.

22
00:01:02,371 --> 00:01:06,306
The EPA counts about seventeen and a half thousand public

23
00:01:06,306 --> 00:01:10,242
wastewater plants in the U.S., serving roughly two hundred seventy

24
00:01:10,242 --> 00:01:13,390
million people, or eighty-two percent of the country.

25
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:16,306
Most of the rest use septic systems.

26
00:01:16,306 --> 00:01:18,892
The EPA says more than one in five

27
00:01:18,892 --> 00:01:21,478
American households rely on one, so their flush

28
00:01:21,478 --> 00:01:24,064
ends in a tank buried in the yard.

29
00:01:24,526 --> 00:01:27,669
For everyone else, the trip is mostly downhill.

30
00:01:27,669 --> 00:01:30,244
Sewer lines are laid on a gentle

31
00:01:30,244 --> 00:01:32,452
slope so gravity does the work.

32
00:01:32,452 --> 00:01:35,264
Where a hill gets in the way, a lift station

33
00:01:35,264 --> 00:01:38,077
pumps the flow up so it can start falling again.

34
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:40,452
How much pipe are we talking about?

35
00:01:40,452 --> 00:01:43,203
The American Society of Civil Engineers puts

36
00:01:43,203 --> 00:01:45,955
it at well over a million miles.

37
00:01:45,955 --> 00:01:50,602
Their latest report card gave America's wastewater system a D-plus.

38
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:52,984
This is where your choices matter.

39
00:01:52,984 --> 00:01:54,356
Pumps hate wipes.

40
00:01:54,356 --> 00:01:58,491
Utilities say even ones sold as flushable can twist together with

41
00:01:58,491 --> 00:02:02,250
hair and grease into thick ropes that jam the pumps.

42
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:03,609
Crews call it ragging.

43
00:02:04,072 --> 00:02:08,071
During 2020, the EPA put out a simple request.

44
00:02:08,071 --> 00:02:10,612
Flush toilet paper and nothing else.

45
00:02:10,612 --> 00:02:12,894
Sewer people put it even shorter.

46
00:02:12,894 --> 00:02:15,163
Pee, poop, and paper.

47
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:16,762
One more wrinkle.

48
00:02:16,762 --> 00:02:19,374
In some older cities, the same pipes

49
00:02:19,374 --> 00:02:21,613
also carry rain from the streets.

50
00:02:21,613 --> 00:02:25,118
ASCE counts more than seven hundred of these combined

51
00:02:25,118 --> 00:02:28,623
systems, and in a heavy storm they can overflow.

52
00:02:29,534 --> 00:02:30,489
Stop two.

53
00:02:30,489 --> 00:02:32,548
The flow reaches the plant, and

54
00:02:32,548 --> 00:02:34,606
the first job is pure muscle.

55
00:02:34,606 --> 00:02:36,128
Get the big stuff out.

56
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:41,340
The water passes through bar screens, basically giant metal combs.

57
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:43,636
Rakes scrape off whatever gets caught.

58
00:02:43,636 --> 00:02:48,677
Rags, wipes, plastic, things that never should have been flushed.

59
00:02:48,677 --> 00:02:51,014
Most of that ends up in a landfill.

60
00:02:51,474 --> 00:02:53,367
Next comes grit removal.

61
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:57,239
Sand, gravel, coffee grounds, eggshells.

62
00:02:57,239 --> 00:03:00,092
Heavy stuff that wears out pumps like sandpaper.

63
00:03:00,092 --> 00:03:03,448
The plant slows the water down just enough for the

64
00:03:03,448 --> 00:03:06,804
grit to drop out, while the lighter material keeps moving.

65
00:03:07,706 --> 00:03:10,944
Stop three is the quietest part of the whole trip.

66
00:03:10,944 --> 00:03:14,574
The water flows into huge round tanks called

67
00:03:14,574 --> 00:03:17,751
primary clarifiers and basically just sits there.

68
00:03:18,208 --> 00:03:20,595
At one sewer district in Utah, it

69
00:03:20,595 --> 00:03:22,642
sits for two to four hours.

70
00:03:22,642 --> 00:03:25,298
Solids sink into a layer of sludge on

71
00:03:25,298 --> 00:03:27,954
the bottom, while grease floats to the top.

72
00:03:27,954 --> 00:03:30,563
Slow-moving arms sweep both away.

73
00:03:31,014 --> 00:03:34,560
The U.S. Geological Survey says this step removes

74
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:37,662
about sixty percent of the suspended solids.

75
00:03:37,662 --> 00:03:40,013
Not bad for doing almost nothing.

76
00:03:40,013 --> 00:03:42,581
But the water is still cloudy and loaded

77
00:03:42,581 --> 00:03:45,149
with dissolved waste you can't just wait out.

78
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:48,070
That dissolved part is the real problem.

79
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:50,294
Dump it into a river, and bacteria

80
00:03:50,294 --> 00:03:52,200
in the river start eating it.

81
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:54,137
As they eat, they use up

82
00:03:54,137 --> 00:03:56,073
the oxygen fish need to breathe.

83
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,238
So how do you clean out something you can't even see?

84
00:03:59,238 --> 00:04:03,382
You run the river's own process inside a tank, on purpose.

85
00:04:03,382 --> 00:04:04,795
You hire an army.

86
00:04:04,795 --> 00:04:08,301
It's alive, it's hungry, and keeping it breathing helps make

87
00:04:08,301 --> 00:04:11,806
sewer plants some of the biggest power users in town.

88
00:04:12,714 --> 00:04:13,792
Stop four.

89
00:04:13,792 --> 00:04:16,013
The water moves into aeration tanks

90
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:18,235
that look like giant hot tubs.

91
00:04:18,235 --> 00:04:21,003
Blowers push air through pipes along the bottom,

92
00:04:21,003 --> 00:04:23,425
and the whole tank churns with bubbles.

93
00:04:23,888 --> 00:04:26,146
In that water lives a dense

94
00:04:26,146 --> 00:04:28,403
crowd of bacteria and other microbes.

95
00:04:28,403 --> 00:04:30,557
They eat the dissolved waste, the same

96
00:04:30,557 --> 00:04:32,710
stuff that would have choked the river.

97
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:35,227
The air is there because these microbes

98
00:04:35,227 --> 00:04:37,384
need oxygen to do it fast.

99
00:04:37,846 --> 00:04:40,663
The basic trick is more than a century old.

100
00:04:40,663 --> 00:04:45,683
In 1914, Edward Ardern and William Lockett, working at a

101
00:04:45,683 --> 00:04:50,201
sewage plant in Manchester, England, presented a simple idea.

102
00:04:50,201 --> 00:04:52,062
Don't throw the microbes away.

103
00:04:52,062 --> 00:04:55,989
Keep the sludge, and the next batch cleans up much faster.

104
00:04:55,989 --> 00:04:58,611
They called it activated sludge, and plants

105
00:04:58,611 --> 00:05:01,233
worldwide still run on versions of it.

106
00:05:01,692 --> 00:05:04,228
After the aeration tank, the water goes

107
00:05:04,228 --> 00:05:06,764
to a second set of settling tanks.

108
00:05:06,764 --> 00:05:10,202
The well-fed microbes clump together and sink.

109
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:12,296
Many of them get pumped right back to

110
00:05:12,296 --> 00:05:14,128
the start to keep the colony going.

111
00:05:14,128 --> 00:05:17,240
That's Ardern and Lockett's idea, still running.

112
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:19,067
That air isn't cheap.

113
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:23,636
The EPA says water and wastewater plants often use thirty to

114
00:05:23,636 --> 00:05:27,789
forty percent of all the energy a city government consumes.

115
00:05:27,789 --> 00:05:31,261
And inside a typical sewer plant, blowing air for

116
00:05:31,261 --> 00:05:34,732
the microbes is usually the single biggest power draw.

117
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:38,463
When this stage is done, the USGS says more

118
00:05:38,463 --> 00:05:41,726
than ninety percent of the suspended solids are gone.

119
00:05:41,726 --> 00:05:44,022
The water is starting to look like water.

120
00:05:44,928 --> 00:05:47,038
Stop five is about germs.

121
00:05:47,038 --> 00:05:50,435
Clean-looking water can still carry bacteria and viruses

122
00:05:50,435 --> 00:05:53,832
that make people sick, so plants disinfect it.

123
00:05:53,832 --> 00:05:55,095
Some use chlorine.

124
00:05:55,095 --> 00:05:58,968
Others shine strong ultraviolet light through it, which

125
00:05:58,968 --> 00:06:02,840
scrambles the microbes' DNA so they can't reproduce.

126
00:06:03,302 --> 00:06:04,719
Chlorine has a catch.

127
00:06:04,719 --> 00:06:08,295
Leftover chlorine is toxic to fish, so plants that use

128
00:06:08,295 --> 00:06:11,513
it often have to remove it again before release.

129
00:06:11,513 --> 00:06:14,611
Then the treated water, called effluent, flows out

130
00:06:14,611 --> 00:06:17,709
to a river, a lake, or the ocean.

131
00:06:18,172 --> 00:06:19,888
It wasn't always like this.

132
00:06:19,888 --> 00:06:24,617
In 1969, the Cuyahoga River in Cleveland caught fire.

133
00:06:24,617 --> 00:06:26,573
And it wasn't even the first time.

134
00:06:26,573 --> 00:06:30,622
That river had been burning on and off since 1868.

135
00:06:31,074 --> 00:06:34,244
That fire became a national symbol of pollution.

136
00:06:34,244 --> 00:06:38,048
Three years later, Congress passed the Clean Water Act, and

137
00:06:38,048 --> 00:06:41,472
cities across the country had to upgrade their plants.

138
00:06:41,936 --> 00:06:45,194
The EPA says that back in 1972, almost

139
00:06:45,194 --> 00:06:48,452
twenty-eight percent of Americans were served by plants

140
00:06:48,452 --> 00:06:51,709
that didn't reach this secondary level of treatment.

141
00:06:51,709 --> 00:06:53,598
Today it's about one percent.

142
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:58,784
And about forty-two percent now get advanced treatment, often extra steps

143
00:06:58,784 --> 00:07:03,069
to strip out nitrogen and phosphorus, which feed algae blooms.

144
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:06,834
Now, remember everything that settled out?

145
00:07:06,834 --> 00:07:09,137
The solids from the first tanks, and

146
00:07:09,137 --> 00:07:11,440
the extra microbes from the bug farm?

147
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:12,649
That's sludge.

148
00:07:12,649 --> 00:07:15,530
And a city's worth of toilets makes a lot of it.

149
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:21,446
Many plants pump it into sealed, heated tanks called anaerobic digesters.

150
00:07:21,446 --> 00:07:23,022
No oxygen this time.

151
00:07:23,022 --> 00:07:26,901
A different crowd of microbes breaks the sludge down, typically

152
00:07:26,901 --> 00:07:30,781
held at around ninety-five degrees for two weeks or more.

153
00:07:31,234 --> 00:07:35,314
In Brooklyn, the Newtown Creek plant does this in eight giant

154
00:07:35,314 --> 00:07:39,024
steel eggs, one of the strangest sights in New York.

155
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:43,026
Those microbes give off biogas, which is mostly

156
00:07:43,026 --> 00:07:46,128
methane, the main ingredient in natural gas.

157
00:07:46,128 --> 00:07:48,506
Plenty of plants burn it to heat

158
00:07:48,506 --> 00:07:50,884
the digesters or make their own electricity.

159
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:53,859
What's left is dewatered and treated,

160
00:07:53,859 --> 00:07:56,368
and the EPA calls it biosolids.

161
00:07:56,368 --> 00:07:59,769
For 2024, the EPA reported that about sixty

162
00:07:59,769 --> 00:08:03,170
percent went onto land, roughly a quarter went

163
00:08:03,170 --> 00:08:06,572
to landfills, and about fourteen percent was burned.

164
00:08:07,036 --> 00:08:10,383
Milwaukee has been doing this since 1926.

165
00:08:10,383 --> 00:08:13,732
Its plant dries the microbes into pellets and

166
00:08:13,732 --> 00:08:17,081
sells them as a lawn fertilizer called Milorganite.

167
00:08:17,081 --> 00:08:20,451
The name comes from Milwaukee Organic Nitrogen.

168
00:08:20,922 --> 00:08:23,521
To be fair, it's not all good news.

169
00:08:23,521 --> 00:08:27,638
Sludge can carry PFAS, the so-called forever chemicals.

170
00:08:27,638 --> 00:08:32,706
In January 2025, the EPA released a draft risk assessment that

171
00:08:32,706 --> 00:08:37,774
flagged possible risks on some farms, even at very low levels.

172
00:08:37,774 --> 00:08:41,212
Maine has already banned spreading biosolids on land.

173
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:44,287
Okay, the California story.

174
00:08:44,287 --> 00:08:47,330
In Orange County, a big share of the treated

175
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:50,374
water skips the ocean and goes through another plant.

176
00:08:50,824 --> 00:08:56,640
The Groundwater Replenishment System pushes it through microfiltration, then reverse

177
00:08:56,640 --> 00:09:01,875
osmosis membranes, then ultraviolet light combined with hydrogen peroxide.

178
00:09:02,334 --> 00:09:06,795
Then the purified water is either injected underground as a barrier

179
00:09:06,795 --> 00:09:11,256
against seawater, or sent to basins where it slowly soaks down

180
00:09:11,256 --> 00:09:14,906
into the same groundwater basin local wells draw from.

181
00:09:15,356 --> 00:09:17,590
The system can produce up to one

182
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:19,504
hundred thirty million gallons a day.

183
00:09:19,504 --> 00:09:23,594
The water district says that's enough for nearly one million people.

184
00:09:24,058 --> 00:09:26,546
So the honest answer to where your flush

185
00:09:26,546 --> 00:09:28,723
goes is, back into the water cycle.

186
00:09:28,723 --> 00:09:29,986
Usually a river.

187
00:09:29,986 --> 00:09:33,288
In a few places, eventually, somebody's tap.

188
00:09:34,194 --> 00:09:35,312
Quick recap.

189
00:09:35,312 --> 00:09:37,704
Pipes carry it downhill to the plant.

190
00:09:37,704 --> 00:09:40,611
Screens and grit traps pull out the junk.

191
00:09:40,611 --> 00:09:42,935
Big tanks let the heavy stuff settle.

192
00:09:42,935 --> 00:09:45,285
Microbes eat what's dissolved.

193
00:09:45,285 --> 00:09:48,179
Chlorine or UV kills the germs.

194
00:09:48,179 --> 00:09:53,234
And the leftovers become fuel, fertilizer, or yes, landfill.

195
00:09:53,696 --> 00:09:56,961
All of it running day and night, mostly out of sight.

196
00:09:56,961 --> 00:10:02,043
ASCE says the average household sewer bill went from about thirty-five

197
00:10:02,043 --> 00:10:06,662
dollars a month in 2010 to nearly sixty-five in 2020.

198
00:10:06,662 --> 00:10:08,238
That's what you're paying for.

199
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:09,869
I'm curious.

200
00:10:09,869 --> 00:10:12,288
Have you ever toured your local treatment plant, or

201
00:10:12,288 --> 00:10:14,706
do you know where your town's water ends up?

202
00:10:14,706 --> 00:10:16,242
Tell me in the comments.

203
00:10:16,692 --> 00:10:19,565
Here on The Civic Machine, we follow everyday

204
00:10:19,565 --> 00:10:22,438
things, a flush, a light switch, a trash

205
00:10:22,438 --> 00:10:25,310
bag, all the way to the other end.

206
00:10:25,310 --> 00:10:28,340
If that's your kind of curiosity, subscribe.

207
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:31,153
Next time, we're following your garbage truck.
